WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.000
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.300
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.580
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:08.840
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.660
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:19.640
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.780
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.140
It's early here today for us.

9
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.640
It's early here, and we have a lot to cover.

10
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.660
So much.

11
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:32.760
We came in early.

12
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.000
We have so much to say today, but I have to point out an observation before we get started.

13
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.220
Is it all my snacks and beverages on my side?

14
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.940
You actually do have a ton of snacks and beverages.

15
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:50.620
You have a hot tea, a cold tea, a water, a granola bar, a banana, and some Naughty Oats cereal.

16
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:51.260
I do.

17
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.460
It's a lot.

18
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.160
You knew this was going to be a big episode.

19
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.160
I came ready.

20
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.120
You've had an entire picnic.

21
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.540
I will always show up with iced tea and snacks.

22
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.300
Just know that.

23
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.080
That's me rolling in to any event.

24
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.840
My observation was actually going to be about your document.

25
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:08.520
Oh.

26
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.980
It's so charming.

27
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.920
It is printed words.

28
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:15.980
It's not note cards.

29
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.300
It is a pile of paper.

30
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.740
But what you have done is you have taken like regular lined.

31
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.200
What's that paper called?

32
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.780
It's called notebook paper out of a spiral binder because clearly I ran out of printer paper.

33
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:30.780
Is that what happened?

34
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:31.940
That's what happened.

35
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.960
That's why your pages are on lined notebook.

36
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.000
It's printed though.

37
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:37.560
Yes.

38
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.580
Did you think that I just wanted to print on notebook paper?

39
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.560
Lady, I didn't know what you were doing.

40
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:44.900
And I have a post-it note.

41
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.220
I just found it interesting when we sat down.

42
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.820
No, this is me.

43
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.100
You know, last night as I print my document out, you might have heard a few expletives

44
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.400
because we were out of printer paper.

45
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:56.800
So then I was like, damn it.

46
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.240
It's nine o'clock at night.

47
00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.520
A good hack.

48
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.280
There you go.

49
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:00.600
Hack.

50
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.180
Go into your kid's room.

51
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.160
Get their notebook.

52
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.260
Rip out the pages.

53
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.160
Your printer will print on it.

54
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.580
I feel like we might have helped some people today.

55
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:09.360
We might have.

56
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.400
All right.

57
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.220
Well, let's talk about why we're here today.

58
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:20.480
It is Stress Relief Part 1, Season 5, Episode 14, written by Paul Lieberstein and directed

59
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.740
by Jeff Blitz.

60
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:22.780
Exactly.

61
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.300
You might be thinking, ladies, it's stress relief.

62
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.400
That isn't that big, except it had the cold open to end all cold opens.

63
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.320
Oh, yeah.

64
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:32.160
Oh, yeah.

65
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.560
We'll be breaking that down.

66
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:34.900
Oh, yeah, we will.

67
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.040
It's going to take a while.

68
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.640
This episode originally aired as an hour-long episode.

69
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.200
We're going to have to break it down in two parts.

70
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.040
So today we're covering the first half of stress relief.

71
00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.340
Would you like a summary?

72
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:49.620
I really would.

73
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:50.300
All right.

74
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:53.060
It starts with that cold open you were talking about, Angela.

75
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.360
Dwight takes matters into his own hands when his co-workers fail to pay attention to his

76
00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.760
fire safety seminar.

77
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.280
He used PowerPoint.

78
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:02.140
Big mistake.

79
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:02.920
Big mistake.

80
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.720
He's going to give us a practical fire drill.

81
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.500
Hands-on.

82
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.680
How will we react on our feet?

83
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:09.280
That's right.

84
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.720
Well, Stanley has a heart attack.

85
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.540
It doesn't end well.

86
00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:13.960
It doesn't.

87
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.500
And that lands Dwight in hot water with corporate.

88
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.880
I mean, he almost burned the building.

89
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.360
Stanley had a heart attack.

90
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.320
Dwight, how do you have a job?

91
00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:30.120
Michael hires a CPR expert to teach the office what we should do if Stanley has another heart

92
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:30.660
attack.

93
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.720
But Dwight has his own ideas of how to use the CPR dummy.

94
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.220
Hello, Clarice.

95
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:36.840
Oh, dear.

96
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.900
Finally, we learned that Pam's mom and dad are having a rough patch in their marriage and

97
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.120
that Pam's dad has actually been bunking with Jim and Pam.

98
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.500
Sans robe.

99
00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:48.560
Yeah.

100
00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.300
Was he walking around in his tighty-whities?

101
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.540
I don't know, but she needs to get him a robe.

102
00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:55.180
Aw.

103
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:04.360
Fast fact number one, this episode aired after the Super Bowl on Sunday, February 1st, 2009.

104
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.840
I mean, if I had an air horn thing, I'd fire it off.

105
00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.580
Because this was huge for us.

106
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.140
Sophia C.

107
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.640
wrote in to say,

108
00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.580
Jenna, I have a fast fact for you.

109
00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:17.140
Sophia.

110
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.860
This was the most watched episode of the entire Office series.

111
00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:23.580
What?

112
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:24.400
Yeah.

113
00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:25.800
And she's right.

114
00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.540
I looked it up.

115
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.840
Angela, do you want to know how many people?

116
00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:30.880
Oh, my gosh.

117
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.460
I'm horrible at guessing.

118
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.900
Like, you know, guess the jelly bean in the jar.

119
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.080
I'm so off.

120
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:36.500
I know.

121
00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.360
Yeah, I can't do that either.

122
00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.580
Okay, I'm going to guess 10 million people.

123
00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.000
That seems like a big number.

124
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.540
That is a big number.

125
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.540
But it was 22.9.

126
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.040
No way!

127
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:51.920
Million people!

128
00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.360
Holy smokes!

129
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:54.260
Yeah.

130
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.480
So it is definitely the most watched episode of the Office.

131
00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.880
And it's also the only episode of the Office to ever reach over 20 million viewers.

132
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:03.960
Wow.

133
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.800
But now I have another question for you.

134
00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:06.440
I don't know.

135
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.860
Is it more guessing number stuff?

136
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:08.740
It's more guessing.

137
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:09.180
Oh, Lord.

138
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.220
It's not numbers, though.

139
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:10.520
Oh, good.

140
00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:11.640
Well, I have a shot.

141
00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.020
Can you guess what our second most watched episode was?

142
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.160
Oh, no.

143
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.020
Of the whole series?

144
00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:21.940
Of the whole series.

145
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.420
This was number one.

146
00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.880
What do you think number two was?

147
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:25.740
The finale?

148
00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.240
The pilot.

149
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:27.920
What?

150
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.440
The pilot.

151
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:30.320
Really?

152
00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:39.520
Mm-hmm.

153
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:40.320
Wow.

154
00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.920
11.2 million people saw your outfit.

155
00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.660
Well, you're welcome, America.

156
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.580
Interestingly enough, airing after the Super Bowl did not seem to get us a bump in viewership

157
00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.220
going forward.

158
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.380
Oh, it was like a one and done?

159
00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.440
Kind of.

160
00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:02.440
So the episode that aired before the Super Bowl was Prince Family Paper, and we had 8.7

161
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:03.200
million viewers.

162
00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.800
That was kind of our average-y range.

163
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:07.080
Right.

164
00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:14.560
After the Super Bowl is lecture circuit, and we actually had a drop in the ratings to 8.4.

165
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:15.360
Oh.

166
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:23.240
So some regular viewers, I don't know, they were like, not after that Super Bowl stunt.

167
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.640
And fast fact number two, Randy told me that there were rumors that we were going to get

168
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.660
that 2009 Super Bowl slot from as early as the end of season four.

169
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:34.760
Jeez.

170
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:35.440
Yeah.

171
00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.060
Did they plan that that far out?

172
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.240
I guess so.

173
00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.240
There had been some talk that maybe it would go to The Apprentice.

174
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.440
The Apprentice was going to get the slot.

175
00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:44.740
Okay.

176
00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.000
But our executive producers really fought for the office to get it, and we did.

177
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:57.080
Randy said it was part of our internal shooting records as of May 2008, which was well before

178
00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.240
we had ever started shooting season five, so we'd known for a while this was coming.

179
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.580
I also reached out to Greg Daniels.

180
00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:04.600
Oh, yeah?

181
00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:05.440
What'd he have to say?

182
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.080
He said this.

183
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.340
Oh, yeah.

184
00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:09.980
This was a big deal.

185
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:10.620
Yeah.

186
00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.120
Quote, we threw our biggest guns at it, which is why Paul Lieberstein wrote it.

187
00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:16.320
That makes sense.

188
00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.360
It's a fantastic script.

189
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.300
I texted Paul yesterday just a few couplets of dialogue.

190
00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.480
I was like, this is perfection.

191
00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.340
I'll tell you what I texted him later.

192
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.800
Well, Paul had been with us since the very beginning.

193
00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.220
He is one of our beSt.

194
00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.600
It made total sense that he should write this episode.

195
00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:32.800
Right.

196
00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:34.320
I got curious.

197
00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:34.880
Okay.

198
00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.940
And I started doing a little digging.

199
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.260
Angela, the script for this episode was 95 pages long.

200
00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:42.860
I know.

201
00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:47.120
Oh, I looked through it and I was like, what is this phone book I'm reading?

202
00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:47.880
Oh, my God.

203
00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.020
It's stress relief.

204
00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:50.280
The candy bag?

205
00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:51.000
Okay.

206
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:51.580
The candy bag.

207
00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:52.280
Our alts?

208
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.640
All of our alt dialogue?

209
00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:54.200
Yeah.

210
00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.940
All the extra jokes that they didn't put in the script but that they want us to maybe shoot.

211
00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:58.820
Oh, my God.

212
00:07:59.020 --> 00:07:59.900
How long was it?

213
00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.000
Lady, it was 207 pages long.

214
00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.400
They had 207 pages of extra jokes.

215
00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:08.720
Yeah.

216
00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.360
On the ready to go.

217
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:10.740
Yep.

218
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:11.620
Oh, my God.

219
00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.800
So I thought we should tell everyone that this episode took us nine days to shoot.

220
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.420
It was a very, very big deal.

221
00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.560
Greg also said that because we were running after the Super Bowl,

222
00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:29.900
it created a scrutiny from the network that was not normally present on our show.

223
00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:30.540
Oh, yeah.

224
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:31.980
Lots of notes.

225
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:33.660
Lots of opinions.

226
00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:34.680
Sure.

227
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.960
This is a big piece of real estate.

228
00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.140
This will come up again later.

229
00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:39.820
Mm-hmm.

230
00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.520
Should I move us to fast fact number three?

231
00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:43.300
Yes.

232
00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:44.320
All right.

233
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.120
Fast fact number three.

234
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:53.080
Greg told us that because there would be so many new eyes on this episode, new viewers,

235
00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:59.240
that they had to figure out a storyline that would appeal to both current office viewers

236
00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.480
and new ones.

237
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:06.580
And they also had to find a way to kind of reintroduce everyone's characters so that

238
00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.120
new viewers could follow along.

239
00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.660
They really tried to stay away from existing storylines.

240
00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:18.300
So, for example, there's no movement or mention of the Angela-Andy-Dwight love triangle.

241
00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:19.000
Right.

242
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.420
There's nothing about the company is struggling and there might be layoffs.

243
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.260
No, there's nothing very complicated.

244
00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.300
This is a very simple idea that can hook everybody.

245
00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:30.220
Yes.

246
00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.160
But they especially had to figure out how do we start this episode?

247
00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.440
How do we hook these new viewers and make them stay for the whole hour?

248
00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:38.960
Right.

249
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.820
So, Greg had this idea.

250
00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:47.240
What if an office weirdo who wanted people to listen to his safety ideas actually caused

251
00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.500
a fire emergency?

252
00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.240
He was like, you don't have to know who's who.

253
00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.660
It has a lot of energy and a lot of physical comedy.

254
00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:57.720
There you go.

255
00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.080
I mean, that is brilliant.

256
00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.860
Greg is brilliant because instantly you're hooked.

257
00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:07.160
Everyone has an office-like idiot that is going to, like, take matters in their own hands

258
00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:07.680
or something.

259
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.480
I thought it was amazing.

260
00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:16.380
Well, we will break down this epic cold open after the break, but I have one more thing

261
00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:16.860
to say.

262
00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:17.740
Okay.

263
00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.780
This is a bonus fast fact.

264
00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:20.160
Okay.

265
00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:22.280
Did you notice the main titles?

266
00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:23.640
Of course I did.

267
00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.620
Everybody is in them.

268
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:26.400
I know.

269
00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.320
This is the first time that our main titles featured the entire caSt.

270
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.740
It was so cool.

271
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.680
We were so excited.

